<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nandivarman II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Pallavamalla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nandivarman II, known as Pallavamalla, was king of the Pallava dynasty from about 731 to 796. When the main line died out he was chosen as a boy from a collateral branch by officials and leading men. He survived the Chalukya occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> around 740 and ruled for some sixty-five years, and he built the Vaikuntha Perumal temple.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the Kasakkudi plates, Nandivarman II descended from Bhimavarman, younger brother of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simhavishnu">Simhavishnu</a>, through a collateral line known as the Kadavas. His father, Hiranyavarman, belonged to the Kadavakula, and Nandivarman himself is described as one who was born to raise the prestige of the Kadava family. Hiranyavarman had four sons, Srimalla, Ranamalla, Samkramalla and Pallavamalla, and it was Pallavamalla who accepted the throne.</p><p>Accounts differ on where the family lived. Inscriptions beneath the panels of the Vaikuntha Perumal temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> say that Hiranyavarman ruled in Kambujadesa, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, with Bhimapura as his capital, while some historians place the branch at Simhapura in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Paramesvaravarman II left no heir, so the ministers, feudatories and advisers of the kingdom sent an expedition to neighbouring states and distant lands to find a suitable prince. They chose the twelve-year-old Pallavamalla, who was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and crowned king of the Pallavas under the name Nandivarman.</p><p>The Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a> helped him recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> by making war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, and around 751 Nandivarman married Reva, Dantidurga's daughter. Besides the Vaikuntha Perumal temple he commissioned the Tiru Parameswara Vinnagaram. The Pallava kings bore the Tamil title Kaduvetti, destroyer or clearer of forests, after their reputed habit, like their ancestor Mukkanti Kaduvetti, of clearing forest land and settling communities on it.</p><p>Nandivarman died in 796, having come to the throne as a boy of twelve. He was succeeded by Dantivarman, his son by Reva, so that the Pallava throne passed to a grandson of the Rashtrakuta emperor who had helped him regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nandivarman II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the main line died out around 731, officials and leading men chose the young <b>Nandivarman II</b> from a collateral branch, an event thought to be shown in the sculptured panels of the Vaikuntha Perumal temple in Kanchipuram.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions show the hymns sung in temples by the 8th century, one of them of <b>Nandivarman II</b>, and later kings made provision for singers of patikams.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tevaram">Tevaram</a></li></ul></section>
